# Detection Rules
This folder contains the custom Wazuh correlation rules developed and tested during this project.

# Brute Force Detection Rule

## Description

This custom Wazuh correlation rule detects repeated failed SSH login attempts from the same source IP address. It helps identify potential brute-force attacks by monitoring authentication failures and generating an alert when the configured threshold is exceeded.

## Detection Logic

- Monitors repeated SSH authentication failures.
- Triggers after *5 failed login attempts within 5 minutes*.
- Uses the *same source IP* to correlate events.
- Mapped to *MITRE ATT&CK T1110 – Brute Force*.

## Rule Configuration

xml
<rule id="100002" level="10" frequency="5" timeframe="300">
  <if_matched_sid>5760, 5712, 5716</if_matched_sid>
  <same_source_ip />
  <description>Possible SSH Brute Force Attack: 5 or more failed logins from the same IP within 5 minutes.</description>
  <mitre>
    <id>T1110</id>
  </mitre>
</rule>

## Test Scenario

A brute-force attack was simulated by repeatedly attempting to log in to the SSH service using invalid credentials from the same source IP address. After five failed authentication attempts within five minutes, the custom correlation rule was triggered.

## Result

The custom Wazuh rule successfully detected the brute-force activity and generated a high-severity alert. This confirmed that the correlation logic correctly identified repeated failed login attempts originating from the same source IP address.

### Rule Configuration

xml
<rule id="100003" level="12">
  <if_sid>5402,5501,1002,2901,2902</if_sid>
  <regex>/tmp/|/var/tmp/</regex>
  <description>Suspicious execution detected: Binary or script executed from a temporary directory (/tmp/)</description>
  <mitre>
    <id>T1059</id>
  </mitre>
</rule>


### Test Scenario

The rule was validated by executing a binary or script from the /tmp/ directory. Wazuh successfully detected the activity and generated a high-severity alert, confirming that the custom detection rule was functioning as intended.

